# The lessons ledger: keep the table's cell padding AS-AUTHORED.
#
# Prettier aligns every cell of a markdown table to the widest cell in its
# column. The ledger's lesson table is ~145 rows whose columns are already
# 2131/856/945 characters wide, so a new row with a wider `evidence` or
# `enforced by` cell re-pads EVERY OTHER ROW: measured with the CI-pinned
# prettier@3.8.1, inserting one row with a 1200-char final cell rewrites 145
# rows and widens the column to 1205 (#1584 did exactly this: 149/145 numstat
# for one new lesson, columns 856->964 and 945->1082).
#
# Two costs, both paid repeatedly:
#   * every ledger PR conflicts with every other ledger PR, for a purely
#     cosmetic reason -- a `+1/-0` change collides with a `+1/-0` change, and
#     the conflict hunk spans the whole table, so a union resolution duplicates
#     all 145 rows. The conflict signal stops carrying any information about
#     whether two ledger PRs are actually compatible.
#   * the one row that is genuinely new is buried in a ~150-line diff.
#
# CI cannot see this churn: each padding width is canonical FOR ITS OWN
# CONTENT, so `--check` exits 0 on the narrow table and on the re-padded one
# alike. Exempting the file is what stops the re-pad; the table stays readable
# because GitHub renders a ragged source table identically.
#
# This also removes the destructive half of #1055 -- prettier hard-wrapping a
# row that sits one blank line adrift from its table, which destroys the lesson
# while every guard stays green. `orphaned_row_problems` still reports the
# adrift row; prettier just no longer eats it first.
#
# Guarded by tests/workflow-logic/test_ledger_is_exempt_from_prettier.py.
docs/lessons-ledger.md
